css-responsive-2.body:
Continue with the same brief: home baker wey wants small cake menu to show well for narrow phones and bigger screens. For this lesson, turn your plan into actual work. Remember the people wey go use am and make one clear choice at a time, so you fit explain why your result useful instead of depending only on template or automatic feature.

Sample details: practice menu cards for vanilla loaf, chocolate cupcakes and coconut slices; long sample label “Celebration-size chocolate cupcakes”. These details na made-up practice examples; replace dem only with material wey belongs to you or wey you get permission to use.

Exercise: Use Grid or Flexbox arrange cards when enough space dey. Grid with repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 15rem), 1fr)) na one way to start; understand each part before you copy am. Set sensible maximum width for content and centre the container inside available space. Add media query only where the content needs different arrangement, instead of writing rules for one phone brand. Allow text wrap naturally, and inspect long names. Give links or buttons visible keyboard-focus style and enough space around their labels.

CSS starter wey you fit run (apply am to container with class menu and cards inside):
* { box-sizing: border-box; }
body { margin: 0; font: 1rem/1.5 sans-serif; padding: 1rem; }
.menu { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 15rem), 1fr)); gap: 1rem; max-width: 65rem; margin-inline: auto; }
.menu > article { padding: 1rem; border: 1px solid #777; }
img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }
a:focus-visible, button:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #174ea6; outline-offset: 3px; }
Try three article cards; resize the browser and check the long cupcake name.

Things to check: Card widths adjust, and the long product label no overlap other content. Write one thing wey you notice before correcting anything, then compare the changed result with the previous version.

Mistake wey people often make: To add separate fixed-width rule for every device.

Keep your evidence: save this stage of the work so you fit explain your choices for the final mini-task. Na original Learning Hub practice this be, no be course or certificate from the software company. The official resource wey the link opens gives extra guidance; software labels and availability fit differ by version.

code-demonstration-2.body:
Continue with the same brief: market cooperative wey dey learn how small JavaScript function adds the prices inside produce orders. For this lesson, turn your plan into actual work. Remember the people wey go use am and make one clear choice at a time, so you fit explain why your result useful instead of depending only on template or automatic feature.

Sample details: sample prices [1200, 800, 500], expected total 2500, empty list wey suppose total 0, and separate example with invalid input. These details na made-up practice examples; replace dem only with material wey belongs to you or wey you get permission to use.

Exercise: Write function sumPrices(prices) with total wey starts at zero, loop wey goes through the prices, and returned total. Run the sample, then follow how total changes from 0 to 1200, 2000 and 2500. Explain why the starting value matters for empty list. Add simple assertion for the example and another one for []. Show wetin happens if string enters the input, then explain whether you go reject invalid values or clearly limit the demonstration to arrays of numbers. No quietly hide result wey you no expect.

JavaScript wey you fit run (paste am inside local console):
function sumPrices(prices) {
  if (!Array.isArray(prices) || prices.some(p => typeof p !== 'number' || !Number.isFinite(p) || p < 0)) throw new TypeError('Use nonnegative numeric prices');
  let total = 0;
  for (const price of prices) total += price;
  return total;
}
console.assert(sumPrices([1200, 800, 500]) === 2500);
console.assert(sumPrices([]) === 0);
console.assert(sumPrices([300, 700]) === 1000);
try { sumPrices([300, '700']); } catch (error) { console.log(error.message); }
This teaching example no be complete library for handling money. Explain the input guard and the test for empty list.

Things to check: The step-by-step trace connects each loop iteration to the final total, and empty input is handled. Write one thing wey you notice before correcting anything, then compare the changed result with the previous version.

Mistake wey people often make: To use big application wey distracts person from the idea wey you dey explain.

Keep your evidence: save this stage of the work so you fit explain your choices for the final mini-task. Na original Learning Hub practice this be, no be course or certificate from the software company. The official resource wey the link opens gives extra guidance; software labels and availability fit differ by version.
